WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.135 --> 00:00:06.637
By the year 2030,

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:12.877
nearly half of all the young people
walking this earth will be African.

00:00:12.877 --> 00:00:17.948
And being able to read is the scale
upon which so many other skills depend.

00:00:18.716 --> 00:00:21.685
Yet, more than 80 percent

00:00:21.719 --> 00:00:24.121
of 10-year-old children
in sub-Saharan Africa

00:00:24.121 --> 00:00:26.957
cannot read a simple text
with understanding.

00:00:26.991 --> 00:00:28.459
Not because they're not smart,

00:00:28.459 --> 00:00:30.561
not because they didn't
pitch up for school,

00:00:30.561 --> 00:00:33.631
but because the system is failing them.

00:00:34.098 --> 00:00:37.368
The go-to solution for this learning
crisis has always been the same:

00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:39.403
build more schools, build more classrooms,

00:00:39.403 --> 00:00:41.739
hire more teachers, train more teachers.

00:00:41.772 --> 00:00:43.207
Sounds reasonable.

00:00:43.941 --> 00:00:48.446
But here’s the uncomfortable truth
no one really wants to say out loud:

00:00:49.647 --> 00:00:51.816
It's actually never going to work.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.519
To meet ideal student-to-teacher ratios,

00:00:55.553 --> 00:01:00.458
sub-Saharan Africa needs
15 million more teachers

00:01:00.491 --> 00:01:06.097
at a staggering cost of almost
$40 billion a year.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.232
It's just nuts.

00:01:08.866 --> 00:01:11.802
So why are we still
holding on to a playbook

00:01:11.836 --> 00:01:15.706
that cannot possibly deliver
at the cost and urgency

00:01:15.739 --> 00:01:17.608
and scale required?

00:01:18.175 --> 00:01:21.011
Well, it's because we have a weird bias.

00:01:21.045 --> 00:01:24.348
Not that kind of weird,
but a WEIRD bias --

00:01:25.116 --> 00:01:29.086
the idea, the assumption
that solutions that work in countries

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.858
that are Western, educated,
industrialized, rich and democratic

00:01:33.891 --> 00:01:36.894
should somehow work
everywhere else in the world.

00:01:37.228 --> 00:01:41.765
(Applause)

00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:46.103
The assumption that WEIRD
countries are the standard,

00:01:46.137 --> 00:01:48.038
"the way the world works,"

00:01:48.072 --> 00:01:49.907
and somehow with more inputs,

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.110
African schools should eventually
look like WEIRD ones.

00:01:53.744 --> 00:01:56.013
Let's pause right there,

00:01:56.046 --> 00:01:58.716
because that assumption
is at the heart of the problem.

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:01.385
And God knows so many others.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.755
We shouldn't have to frame
our world like that.

00:02:04.755 --> 00:02:07.291
And I certainly have outgrown the belief

00:02:07.324 --> 00:02:09.793
that African schools
should fit a borrowed model.

00:02:09.793 --> 00:02:12.930
Our goal should be to build
something brilliant

00:02:12.963 --> 00:02:17.501
that actually reflects the reality
that African children are living in today.

00:02:17.501 --> 00:02:21.539
At the organization I lead,
it’s called “Imagine Worldwide,”

00:02:21.572 --> 00:02:23.774
we are doing something different.

00:02:24.141 --> 00:02:29.547
Children like Chisomo,
who you will hear about shortly,

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.550
are now able to experience something else.

00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:37.821
Chisomo is a typical seven-year-old girl
living in rural Malawi

00:02:37.821 --> 00:02:41.091
with her younger sister
and parents who are farmers.

00:02:41.091 --> 00:02:42.693
It's a pretty happy existence,

00:02:42.726 --> 00:02:45.029
even without iPhones and ketchup.

00:02:45.029 --> 00:02:46.297
(Laughter)

00:02:46.297 --> 00:02:50.100
Every morning, she walks
through the green maize fields

00:02:50.134 --> 00:02:53.237
under the glory of the African sun.

00:02:54.104 --> 00:02:57.541
But at school, she shares a class
with a hundred other kids,

00:02:57.575 --> 00:03:01.045
sometimes 200 -- if a teacher was absent.

00:03:01.078 --> 00:03:04.248
She can barely see the book
that the teacher was holding up

00:03:04.281 --> 00:03:07.618
in an overcrowded, dimly lit room.

00:03:07.618 --> 00:03:10.588
She rarely gets a chance
to ask a question, share an answer,

00:03:10.621 --> 00:03:13.123
get feedback -- and feedback
is everything.

00:03:14.358 --> 00:03:16.360
I remember when I tutored
high-school math,

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.496
some kids didn’t even get
their marked tests back --

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.031
just a score.

00:03:21.365 --> 00:03:24.802
So they never knew where they went wrong

00:03:24.802 --> 00:03:26.604
or right, for that matter.

00:03:27.171 --> 00:03:28.973
There was no feedback loop,

00:03:29.006 --> 00:03:32.810
just this vacuum of uncertainty
that makes it impossible

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.246
to get better at anything.

00:03:36.313 --> 00:03:39.316
And [at] Imagine, we are giving
children like Chisomo

00:03:39.316 --> 00:03:43.921
up to an hour a day of high-impact
learning on a tablet.

00:03:44.922 --> 00:03:48.425
I know, I know, I can feel
the parents in the room cringing.

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:51.228
We are so allergic to the idea of screens,

00:03:51.262 --> 00:03:52.763
they are bad for mental health,

00:03:52.763 --> 00:03:54.365
hurting attention spans.

00:03:54.398 --> 00:03:57.534
Really not good for your mental
and emotional development.

00:03:57.568 --> 00:03:59.703
I get that, and that's all true.

00:04:00.371 --> 00:04:02.406
In a few contexts.

00:04:02.773 --> 00:04:06.243
And this is why context is so important.

00:04:06.577 --> 00:04:10.347
Chisomo is not living the WEIRD childhood

00:04:10.381 --> 00:04:13.250
that so many of us are trying
to protect our children from.

00:04:13.584 --> 00:04:16.620
Her reality is not too much tech.

00:04:16.654 --> 00:04:21.091
It's more closer to none at all,
at school or at home.

00:04:22.426 --> 00:04:24.228
The solution that we are delivering

00:04:24.228 --> 00:04:27.798
is really designed to tackle
some of the hardest things

00:04:27.831 --> 00:04:30.834
that EdTech has not been able
to deliver in Africa.

00:04:30.868 --> 00:04:32.236
And I know that many of you

00:04:32.269 --> 00:04:35.272
are very skeptical about tech
in poor countries.

00:04:37.007 --> 00:04:41.045
Our solution is tackling
those issues head on.

00:04:41.578 --> 00:04:44.515
All those prior failures
that were too hardware-focused

00:04:44.548 --> 00:04:47.451
relied on power and connectivity.

00:04:47.484 --> 00:04:49.553
The ones that were cultural misfits,

00:04:49.586 --> 00:04:51.422
lacking in sound pedagogy,

00:04:51.455 --> 00:04:53.357
working outside government systems,

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:56.226
or just plain too expensive to run.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.699
All of those, we have figured
out a model that works.

00:05:02.333 --> 00:05:03.667
Ready to hear it?

00:05:03.701 --> 00:05:07.438
(Cheers and applause)

00:05:07.738 --> 00:05:10.674
Specially designed tablets
that are solar-charged

00:05:10.674 --> 00:05:12.976
are used throughout the school day,

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.612
and they're designed with a battery
that can carry over

00:05:15.612 --> 00:05:18.148
and they can be rotated
throughout classes.

00:05:18.148 --> 00:05:20.384
The content is contextually relevant

00:05:20.417 --> 00:05:23.921
and delivered in local languages
like Swahili and Chichewa,

00:05:23.921 --> 00:05:27.491
including verbal instruction
via the headphones.

00:05:28.292 --> 00:05:30.661
And it works entirely offline.

00:05:30.694 --> 00:05:33.430
No internet, no power, no problem.

00:05:33.464 --> 00:05:36.567
It's not a replacement for teachers,
but an extra helping hand.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.837
The kids only spend about an hour
of their day on it anyway.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.205
But in that hour,

00:05:41.238 --> 00:05:43.640
the tablet does something incredible:

00:05:44.007 --> 00:05:47.911
It adapts to each child's level and pace,

00:05:47.945 --> 00:05:49.880
guiding even first-time users,

00:05:49.913 --> 00:05:54.785
children who are not yet able to read
and have never seen a device,

00:05:54.818 --> 00:05:56.653
from their very first tap,

00:05:56.687 --> 00:06:01.358
all the way to literacy and numeracy --
without adult intervention.

00:06:02.359 --> 00:06:04.328
Imagine what that means

00:06:04.361 --> 00:06:08.932
in a classroom where individual
attention is almost impossible.

00:06:08.966 --> 00:06:13.070
Each child suddenly gets
something incredibly rare:

00:06:13.737 --> 00:06:17.174
instruction that responds to them.

00:06:17.941 --> 00:06:21.011
The child who was struggling
can slow down and get more practice.

00:06:21.044 --> 00:06:24.114
The child who is ready
to keep going can rush ahead.

00:06:25.015 --> 00:06:30.020
Imagine in a classroom where 20 kids
are each on their own tablets, or 200 --

00:06:30.053 --> 00:06:33.190
there is no loss in learning quality.

00:06:33.891 --> 00:06:36.894
This is all achieved
without having to retrain

00:06:36.927 --> 00:06:40.664
or restructure the entire
education system.

00:06:40.697 --> 00:06:44.134
What we're seeing here
is greater access to learning

00:06:44.168 --> 00:06:47.204
and better learning by the minute.

00:06:47.237 --> 00:06:49.506
This is unheard of in education.

00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:52.309
97 percent of teachers in Malawi

00:06:52.342 --> 00:06:55.045
have confirmed that the tablet program

00:06:55.045 --> 00:06:57.314
makes teaching more enjoyable.

00:06:59.149 --> 00:07:03.687
(Applause)

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:08.125
And the results speak for themselves.

00:07:08.492 --> 00:07:11.195
Nine randomized controlled trials

00:07:11.228 --> 00:07:15.532
across four countries in three languages
over the past decade

00:07:15.566 --> 00:07:18.168
have proven that time-bound,

00:07:18.202 --> 00:07:22.272
fit-for-context digital learning
embedded in public school systems

00:07:22.306 --> 00:07:26.410
is the most scalable way
to accelerate literacy

00:07:26.443 --> 00:07:30.214
and numeracy outcomes
for millions of African children.

00:07:30.214 --> 00:07:32.216
We are seeing learning gains

00:07:32.249 --> 00:07:35.152
that rank among the top
education interventions

00:07:35.152 --> 00:07:36.920
anywhere in the world.

00:07:38.989 --> 00:07:44.127
(Cheers and applause)

00:07:44.161 --> 00:07:47.831
Three years ago, we were already
doubling the number of children

00:07:47.831 --> 00:07:51.134
that become literate and numerate
by their fourth year in school.

00:07:51.168 --> 00:07:53.704
Now we're on track to triple that.

00:07:54.571 --> 00:07:56.673
(Applause and cheers)

00:07:56.707 --> 00:07:57.841
I think so, too.

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:01.078
Beyond test scores,

00:08:01.111 --> 00:08:04.815
we're seeing more children
pitch up and stay in school.

00:08:04.848 --> 00:08:08.719
Girls catch up and keep pace with boys.

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:10.320
And the most important thing,

00:08:10.354 --> 00:08:14.091
more children are falling
in love with learning.

00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:17.995
Imagine takes a system-
strengthening approach,

00:08:18.028 --> 00:08:21.031
working in partnership
with government from day one

00:08:21.064 --> 00:08:24.535
to embed the program
within public education systems,

00:08:24.568 --> 00:08:28.405
ensuring a pathway
for scale and sustainability.

00:08:29.172 --> 00:08:30.474
And through that process,

00:08:30.507 --> 00:08:33.677
we're able to create road maps
for others to replicate

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.445
in more countries in the future.

00:08:36.413 --> 00:08:43.053
The best news is this testimony
from this innocent girl where she says,

00:08:43.086 --> 00:08:47.958
"I like the tablet
because when I make a mistake,

00:08:47.991 --> 00:08:50.394
it doesn't move on to the next student.

00:08:50.928 --> 00:08:55.499
It asks me to try again,
and then I can get it right.”

00:08:56.633 --> 00:09:01.038
Finally, a school experience
where children experience success

00:09:01.071 --> 00:09:04.007
and the dignity of real learning.

00:09:04.741 --> 00:09:08.645
This year, Imagine
will reach one million children.

00:09:09.313 --> 00:09:15.085
(Cheers and applause)

00:09:15.118 --> 00:09:19.122
And by 2030, thanks
to the generosity of this community,

00:09:19.156 --> 00:09:23.160
we aim to serve over four
million children a year

00:09:23.193 --> 00:09:26.897
across Malawi, Sierra Leone and Tanzania

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.900
and in the process, create a pathway

00:09:29.933 --> 00:09:32.169
for many, many others to follow.

00:09:32.202 --> 00:09:36.840
This, this right here
is how you turn a tide.

00:09:37.174 --> 00:09:38.909
With a model that can deliver

00:09:38.942 --> 00:09:42.879
at the scale, quality, cost
and urgency required.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.883
Imagine a future
where foundational learning

00:09:46.917 --> 00:09:49.753
that is of high quality is democratized,

00:09:49.753 --> 00:09:52.789
and the majority
of every country's children

00:09:52.823 --> 00:09:54.658
can learn to read

00:09:54.691 --> 00:09:58.228
so that they too can read to learn

00:09:58.261 --> 00:10:02.099
and secure a future in skilled work.

00:10:02.866 --> 00:10:04.067
We don't need to wait.

00:10:04.101 --> 00:10:05.802
We simply need to act and now.

00:10:05.802 --> 00:10:08.338
That future is already knocking,

00:10:08.338 --> 00:10:12.175
sounding the name of every African child.

00:10:12.776 --> 00:10:16.680
(Swahili, Chichewa) Thank you.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.018
(Cheers and applause)

